# Anakata Nuxt apps — rules

## Stack
Nuxt 4 (`app/` directory), Nuxt UI v4, Tailwind CSS v4, TypeScript strict, pnpm.
Every app **extends the `anakata-ui` layer**, which owns the design tokens, the Nuxt UI theme, fonts, shared components (`Ank*`), `useApi()`, `useMoney()`, `useDates()` and the generated API types. Never redefine tokens or fonts inside an app — change the layer instead. The layer's Nuxt UI overrides match the back office (the panel; RMS and CRM prototypes share identical CSS). **The engine differs** (page background `--forest`, larger buttons: 11px / .24em / 15px 32px, easing curves), so anakata-engine adds its own overrides in its `app.config.ts`, which Nuxt merges over the layer's.

| App | Rendering | Users |
|---|---|---|
| anakata-panel | `ssr: false` (SPA) | staff (RMS + CRM), Sanctum cookie session |
| anakata-engine | SSR | public guests; SEO matters |

## Code
- `<script setup lang="ts">` only. Composition API. No Options API, no `any`.
- anakata-panel: see `panel.mdc` for its structure. anakata-engine: pages in `app/pages`, feature components in `app/components/<step-or-area>/`.
- Data fetching only through the layer's `useApi()` (`useFetch`/`$fetch` wrappers with credentials, base URL and CSRF handled). Types come from the layer's generated OpenAPI types — never hand-write API response types.
- State: composables + `useState`. Use Pinia only for large multi-step state (the engine's booking flow).
- UI strings go through i18n (`en` only). Content that the RMS publishes (engine copy, itinerary text, fees, guest rules) comes from the API, never from i18n files.

## No business logic in the frontend
- Never compute an authoritative price, availability, deposit, fee, commission or status. Render what the API returns.
- The engine may compute a **preview** price locally for speed, but must re-request the server price before any submission, and display the server result.
- Permission-aware UI (hide/disable what a role can't do) is allowed, but the API is the enforcement — always handle 403/409/422 gracefully with the server's message.

## Design fidelity — the prototypes are the spec
- Before building a screen, open its prototype HTML and screenshot. Match it exactly: layout, spacing, font family/size/weight/letter-spacing, colours, borders, hover/active/disabled states, empty states.
- Use Nuxt UI components (themed by the layer) for standard controls: buttons, inputs, selects, tables, tabs, modals, slideovers, toasts, badges, dropdowns.
- Build custom components when the prototype element is bespoke (deck plan, calendar grid, invoice/document layouts, route map, itinerary cards, departure rows). Port the prototype's CSS for those using the layer's CSS variables (`--forest`, `--coral`, `--ivory`, `--iv62`, `--iv38`, `--hair`, `--sand`, `--olive`, `--ok`, `--warn`).
- Square corners, hairline borders, uppercase IBM Plex Mono labels, Oswald headings, Archivo body. No shadows, no rounded corners, no Nuxt UI default colours.
- Check every screen in dark (default) and light theme. The engine must also work at phone width and respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

## Quality gate
`pnpm lint`, `pnpm typecheck` and `pnpm build` must pass locally. Apps extend the layer via `../anakata-ui` for now; how they fetch it remotely is decided later with the git host.
